<ProductionLayout title="Research — Skyhook Industries" description="Primary research and flight heritage behind rotating momentum-exchange tethers, electrodynamic reboost, tether deployment and payload capture.">
  <section class="page-hero paper-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research record</p>
    <h1>What flew. What was studied. What remains.</h1>
    <p>The useful question is not whether “space tethers” exist as an idea. It is which parts of a transportation-scale Skyhook have already been demonstrated, which were analyzed in serious aerospace studies, and which still belong to the engineering program ahead.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">01 / Flight heritage</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Tethers have flown in orbit.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The flight record is real but specific. It demonstrates deployment, dynamics, electrodynamic interaction and tether-assisted momentum exchange—not the complete high-speed capture-and-reuse system proposed here.</p>
        <div class="fact-table">
          <div class="fact-row"><b>SEDS-1 / 1993</b><p>NASA’s Small Expendable Deployer System successfully deployed a 20 km tether and used it to deorbit an end mass.</p><small>Flight heritage</small></div>
          <div class="fact-row"><b>SEDS-2 / 1994</b><p>A second 20 km deployment demonstrated controlled deployment and local-vertical stabilization before the tether was severed 3.7 days later.</p><small>Flight heritage</small></div>
          <div class="fact-row"><b>TSS-1R / 1996</b><p>The Tethered Satellite System Reflight deployed about 19.7 km of conducting tether. NASA measured roughly 3.5 kV and 480 mA before the tether failed.</p><small>Flight heritage</small></div>
          <div class="fact-row"><b>YES2 / 2007</b><p>ESA’s Young Engineers’ Satellite 2 deployed essentially its full ~32 km tether. Post-flight orbital evidence indicated that the Fotino capsule was released and entered the atmosphere, although the capsule was not recovered.</p><small>Flight heritage</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">02 / Transportation studies</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Researchers studied the machine, not just the tether.</h2>
        <p class="body-copy">HASTOL examined a hypersonic aircraft rendezvousing with a rotating tether at the edge of the atmosphere. MXER studies treated a momentum-exchange tether as a reusable upper-stage facility and coupled payload capture to electrodynamic reboost. Hoyt and Uphoff developed a separate cislunar tether architecture and formalized damage-tolerant multiline tether structures.</p>
        <p class="body-copy">These studies are valuable because they expose the coupled problem: orbit, rotation, material strength, capture, payload mass, reboost and facility mass cannot be solved independently. They are not proof that Skyhook Industries’ current ~4,000 km study configuration is already closed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">03 / Primary sources</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Start with the reports.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The site links directly to primary or institutional sources wherever possible. Each source below is included for a specific reason.</p>
        <div class="source-list">
          <a class="source" href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/355Bogar.pdf">
            <span class="source-type">NIAC / Boeing</span><div><h3>Hypersonic Airplane Space Tether Orbital Launch System — HASTOL Phase I</h3><p>Atmospheric rendezvous, grapple concepts, materials and tether sizing. Its selected baseline used a 100 km rendezvous and 4.1 km/s inertial vehicle/tip speed with a 600 km tether.</p></div><span class="source-year">2000 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/391Grant.pdf">
            <span class="source-type">NIAC / Boeing</span><div><h3>Hypersonic Airplane Space Tether Orbital Launch — HASTOL Phase II</h3><p>Architecture sizing, simulations, airplane trades, tether boost-facility concepts and follow-on recommendations. It explicitly identifies facility mass and rendezvous/capture as major system drivers.</p></div><span class="source-year">2001 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20020068839">
            <span class="source-type">NASA NTRS</span><div><h3>Orbital Propagation of Momentum Exchange Tether Systems</h3><p>Models the MXER facility as a momentum-exchange system that gives Δv to a payload, loses orbital energy, and then reboosts electrodynamically.</p></div><span class="source-year">2002 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20060005548">
            <span class="source-type">NASA / Lockheed</span><div><h3>Design Concept for a Reusable/Propellantless MXER Tether Space Transportation System</h3><p>Identifies survivable high-strength tether systems and reliable lightweight catch/release mechanisms as major technology areas.</p></div><span class="source-year">2005 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20070013898">
            <span class="source-type">NASA NTRS</span><div><h3>Electrodynamic Bare Tether Systems as a Thruster for the MXER Project</h3><p>Examines electrodynamic tether design and performance as the recovery/reboost element of a momentum-exchange facility.</p></div><span class="source-year">2006 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/7Hoyt.pdf">
            <span class="source-type">NIAC / AIAA</span><div><h3>Cislunar Tether Transport System — Hoyt & Uphoff</h3><p>A separate two-tether Earth–Moon transportation architecture; also relevant for rotating-tether mechanics and damage-tolerant tether concepts.</p></div><span class="source-year">1999 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19980018321/downloads/19980018321.pdf?attachment=true">
            <span class="source-type">NASA</span><div><h3>Tethers in Space Handbook</h3><p>Flight history, tether dynamics, deployers, electrodynamics, materials and multiline/Hoytether concepts.</p></div><span class="source-year">1997 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19970027081">
            <span class="source-type">NASA MSFC</span><div><h3>Space Tethers: Design Criteria</h3><p>Strength, dynamic behavior, materials, electrical conductors, micrometeoroid/debris sever risk, safety and reliability criteria for tether design.</p></div><span class="source-year">1997 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19870001511">
            <span class="source-type">NASA CR</span><div><h3>Guidebook for Analysis of Tether Applications</h3><p>First-order orbit mechanics, gravity-gradient effects, momentum transfer, rendezvous/release, hazards and electrodynamic tether principles.</p></div><span class="source-year">1985 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20060004770/downloads/20060004770.pdf">
            <span class="source-type">NASA</span><div><h3>Synopsis of Major Tether Missions</h3><p>Compact NASA flight record for SEDS-1, SEDS-2, PMG, TSS-1R and TiPS, including deployment lengths and electrodynamic measurements.</p></div><span class="source-year">Flight record ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/sts-75/">
            <span class="source-type">NASA</span><div><h3>STS-75 / TSS-1R mission record</h3><p>NASA mission summary reporting the tether deployment, 3,500 V potential and roughly 480 mA current observed during TSS-1R.</p></div><span class="source-year">1996 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://www.esa.int/Space_in_Member_States/Denmark/Laengste_struktur_i_rummet">
            <span class="source-type">ESA</span><div><h3>YES2 post-flight assessment</h3><p>ESA’s post-flight account: at least 29.5 km and probably the full 31.7 km deployed; the carrier orbit changed as expected when Fotino was released, and tracking indicated the capsule did not remain in orbit.</p></div><span class="source-year">2007 ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="source" href="https://standards.nasa.gov/standard/NASA/NASA-STD-871914">
            <span class="source-type">NASA Standard</span><div><h3>NASA-STD-8719.14C — Process for Limiting Orbital Debris</h3><p>Requires tether debris assessments to address sever probability, collisions, fragment orbital lifetime and disposal. This is directly relevant to sectional isolation and intentional-sever concepts.</p></div><span class="source-year">2021 ↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">04 / What is not demonstrated</p>
      <div>
        <h2>The missing experiment is the transportation loop.</h2>
        <p class="lede">No flight program has yet demonstrated the full sequence that matters for a reusable atmospheric-access Skyhook.</p>
        <div class="hard-list">
          <div class="hard-row"><b>01</b><span>HIGH-SPEED VEHICLE RENDEZVOUS WITH A ROTATING TETHER TIP</span></div>
          <div class="hard-row"><b>02</b><span>CAPTURE AND LOAD TRANSFER INTO A TRANSPORTATION-SCALE STRENGTH TETHER</span></div>
          <div class="hard-row"><b>03</b><span>LARGE MOMENTUM EXCHANGE FOLLOWED BY CONTROLLED PAYLOAD RELEASE</span></div>
          <div class="hard-row"><b>04</b><span>ORBITAL/ROTATIONAL RECOVERY AND REPEAT OPERATION</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="body-copy">That gap is not an embarrassment to hide in the footer. It is the development program.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">05 / Claim standard</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Four labels. No blurred boundaries.</h2>
        <div class="sequence">
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>A</b><strong>FLIGHT HERITAGE</strong><span>Measured or demonstrated in space.</span></div>
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>B</b><strong>PRIOR RESEARCH</strong><span>Modeled or analyzed in credible aerospace literature.</span></div>
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>C</b><strong>SKYHOOK STUDY</strong><span>A present architectural choice being explored by Skyhook Industries.</span></div>
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>D</b><strong>OPEN TARGET</strong><span>A number or feature that still depends on modeling, testing or material performance.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
